= Abstract
This paper investigates the hypothesis that male victims of domestic violence experience systemic legal, economic, and social neglect @verma_nd. Drawing upon a comparative synthesis of qualitative data from Nepal @pun2020 , doctrinal legal analysis from India @verma_nd , and judicial registries from Ukraine @pantaliienko2025, the research demonstrates exactly how male victims get pushed to the margins. The neglect manifests through explicit statutory exclusion (*de jure* neglect) and institutional bias within formally gender-neutral frameworks (*de facto* invisibility) @verma_nd. Ultimately, current legal paradigms disproportionately trap male victims in abusive environments rather than offering them an exit.

= Introduction
Domestic violence is a global human rights violation. For decades, legislative frameworks and epidemiological research have almost entirely centered on violence against women @sapkota2024. While this focus has been crucial for protecting uniquely vulnerable populations, it accidentally built a massive systemic blind spot regarding male and non-binary victims @verma_nd. Contemporary legal and sociological analyses show a harsh reality: male victims are frequently abandoned by the justice system, trapped by punitive economic policies, and silenced by strict cultural scripts @pun2020.

= The "Tyranny of Masculinity" and Social Invisibility
Before we even look at the law, we have to look at society. The foremost barrier to addressing male domestic violence is the expectation of hegemonic masculinity @verma_nd. Male victimization directly contradicts traditional cultural scripts that demand stoicism, dominance, and invulnerability @pun2020. 

Qualitative research from Nepal underscores this deeply. In heavily male-dominated societies, men hesitate to admit they've been violated by a woman because the fear of social ridicule is overwhelming @pun2020. This cultural expectation operates as a "tyranny of masculinity," effectively forcing victims to stay quiet @verma_nd. We see a similar dynamic in Ukraine, where the stereotypical model of the "strong man" exerts immense psychological pressure on victims, deterring them from reporting abuse and severely distorting national crime statistics @pantaliienko2025.

= De Jure Neglect: Explicit Statutory Exclusion
Social invisibility translates directly into legal exclusion. India provides the starkest example of *de jure* neglect. The Protection of Women from Domestic Violence Act, 2005 (PWDVA) explicitly defines an "aggrieved person" exclusively as a woman @verma_nd. 

This strict gender-binary language categorically bars men from accessing essential civil remedies, like protection orders, residence rights, and monetary relief @verma_nd. While men might try to seek recourse under general penal codes for isolated acts of physical violence, these provisions fail miserably at addressing the continuous, relational nature of domestic abuse @verma_nd. The tragic 2024 suicide of Atul Subhash---who faced prolonged emotional abuse and legal harassment via false complaints under Section 498A of the IPC without access to statutory domestic violence protections---highlights the fatal consequences of this legislative void @verma_nd.

= De Facto Invisibility and Economic Coercion
Even when laws are written to be gender-neutral, male victims still face profound *de facto* neglect through institutional apathy and economic entrapment.

== Implementation Bias in Ukraine
In Ukraine, domestic violence is criminalized under Article 126-1 of the Criminal Code, a formally gender-neutral statute @pantaliienko2025. Yet, Pantaliienko (2025) demonstrates that men remain virtually invisible in judicial practice @pantaliienko2025. An analysis of the Unified State Register of Court Decisions reveals that male victims make up a vast minority in court verdicts @pantaliienko2025. Furthermore, 60% of documented abuse against men is psychological, and 15% is economic @pantaliienko2025. Because these forms of violence leave no physical scars, they are inherently harder to prove and much easier for law enforcement to brush off @pantaliienko2025.

== Economic Entrapment in Nepal
Male victims also face severe economic deterrents that coerce them into remaining in abusive marriages. In Nepal, property laws dictate that a woman is legally entitled to half of the marital property upon divorce @pun2020. Qualitative focus groups reveal that Nepali men view the threat of divorce---and the absolute financial destitution that follows it---as a potent form of economic violence perpetrated by abusive spouses @pun2020. Consequently, men are financially coerced into tolerating abuse.

= The Epistemological Gap
The neglect of male victims is compounded by a massive epistemological gap in academic and institutional research. As Sapkota et al. (2024) point out in their systematic review of Nepal, large-scale quantitative data on domestic violence (like the NDHS) almost exclusively measures female victimization @sapkota2024. Because male victims are legally excluded and socially stigmatized, institutions simply do not collect the data necessary to quantify the scope of male suffering @pantaliienko2025. 

= Conclusion
The synthesis of the provided literature definitively proves that male victims of domestic violence are systemically neglected @verma_nd. Trapped by a triad of cultural stigma, economic coercion, and statutory exclusion, men are denied the fundamental human right to a life free from violence @pun2020. True legal equity demands the amendment of exclusionary statutes, the establishment of male-inclusive support@sapkota2024 infrastructure, and the eradication of the institutional biases@verma_nd that perpetuate the invisibility of male victims @pantaliienko2025.

